\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds end firmer but T-bills ease on C$. \b0\par\par\uc2 308 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Dec 22 (Reuters) - The Canadian yield curve flattened in light dealings on Monday, with the short end closing negative as the Canadian dollar traded at almost 12-year lows. \par \par \uc2  "Things just drifted down during the day," said Richard Wylie, Toronto-Dominion Securities fixed-income economist. "The people are a little bit disappointed about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the Bank of Canada isn't going to do anything in defense of it." \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year benchmark bond added C$0.16 to C$127.82 to yield 5.979 percent following its U.S. counterpart higher, as it rose 15/32 to yield 5.89 percent. \par \par \uc2  The positive spread between the two bonds rose to nine basis points from seven at the close of trading on Friday. \par \par \uc2  By contrast the three-month when-issued T-bill softened to yield 4.53 percent from 4.47 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at C$1.4342 (US$0.6973) after repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this morning. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold US$50 million blocks to buy Canadian dollars several times after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped as low as C$1.4353 (US$0.6967), levels not seen since February 1986. \par \par \uc2  More T-bill supply also dampened the short end, said Michael Gregory, Lehman Brothers economist. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada auctioned C$5.8 billion of T-bills, including C$3.6 billion in new cash. The average yield of three-month treasury bills was 4.460 percent, up from 3.799 percent at the last auction. \par \par \uc2  Trading was light ahead of the holidays, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 10-year benchmark bonds rose C$0.05 to C$111.40 to yield 5.668 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Lydia Zajc, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8109)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcm00upa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
