\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds, T-bills end higher in thin trading. \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Dec 19 (Reuters) - Canadian long-term bonds and short-end T-Bills end higher in skimpy pre-holiday trading on Friday, resisting the pull of the weaker Canadian dollar as it dropped to 11-year lows. \par \par \uc2  "Bills held in surprisingly well," said Jim Webber, director of fixed-income research at Toronto-Dominion Securities. Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the week at its weakest closing levels in 11 years, finishing at C$1.4312 (US$0.6987), softer from its close on Thursday of C$1.4265 (US$0.7010). \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month cash T-bill rose to yield 4.47 percent from 4.51 percent at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year benchmark bond rose C$0.41 to C$127.70 to yield 5.987 percent. The U.S. 30-year bond rose 7/32 to yield 5.92 percent. The positive spread between the two bonds closed steady at seven basis points from the close of trading on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Bonds and T-bills drifted higher in the afternoon following a firmer U.S. market, after T-bills opened softer due to a tumbling Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Investors sold the dollar after the Bank of Canada failed to hike short-term interest rates this morning as some expected. \par \par \uc2  "The case for a rate hike was not all that strong this morning with CPI (consumer price index) the way it was," Webber said. Statistics Canada reported that the Canadian consumer price index rose by just 0.9 percent on the year, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not justify increasing rates to cool an overheated economy. Instead the bank tried vainly to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily. \par \par \uc2  Dealings tapered off late in the day. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 10-year benchmark bonds rose C$0.27 to C$111.35 to yield 5.679 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Lydia Zajc, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8109)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcj00s7m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
