\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Occurs As BOC Leaves Rates Flat \b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 1997\par 15:15\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday, selling the U.S.\par dollar heavily starting at C$1.4285 and keeping it up as the\par U.S. dollar burst through the C$1.4300 level, said a trader.\par    The move occured immediately after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par failed to raise interest rates in usual operating window\par just before 9:00 a.m. EST (1400 GMT).\par    Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been under heavy speculative\par attack in recent weeks, and the bank raised its bank rate\par 1/2 point to 4.50% last Friday in its defense. But that\par increase failed to halt the U.S. dollar's rise, and players\par began retargeting levels over C$1.4290, the 11.5-year high\par reached Thursday last week.\par    Observers say the market is now targeting a U.S. dollar\par level at C$1.4350, with a longer-term goal at C$1.4480, the\par all-time high set in February of 1986.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS  12-19-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  0915EST \par \par \uc2  4020 4300 4340 4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtcj02riu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
