\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks shed early gains to close down. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lydia Zajc \par \uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 1997\par 23:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Reuters) - Toronto's key stock index reversed earlier gains to end weaker on Tuesday, pressured by increasing fears of another short-term interest rate hike as the Canadian dollar tumbled. \par \par \uc2  The Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index dipped 17.27 points to close at 6567.43, while the Dow Jones Industrial Average gained almost 54 points, finishing at 7976. \par \par \uc2  "It was a topsy-turvy day," said John Ing, president of the Maison Placements Canada brokerage. \par \par \uc2  The heavyweight banking sector and interest-sensitive issues slid after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars, but the unit still ended lower at 70.15 U.S. cents from Monday's close of 70.40 U.S. cents. The Bank of Canada last boosted interest rates on Friday by 50 points. \par \par \uc2  Golds lost their earlier luster after posting a modest rally, Ing added. The influential sector closed just 0.5 percent higher, failing to hold an earlier 1.8 percent gain. \par \par \uc2  Nine of the 14 sectors lost ground, led by media, real estate, conglomerates and banks. \par \par \uc2  The firmer sub-indexes included industrial products, golds and pipelines. \par \par \uc2  Trading value totaled C$1.6 billion ($1.1 billion) on 109 million shares. Declining issues outnumbered advances 582 to 471, and 325 closed flat. \par \par \uc2  In individual issues, Ballard Power Systems Inc. extended Monday's gains, rising 4.30 to 101.90 after Monday's news that Ford Motor Co. acquired a 15 percent stake in the Canadian fuel cell technology company. Germany's Daimler-Benz AG reduced its 25 percent stake to 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce slipped 0.20 to 46.10 in heavy turnover of 1.9 million shares. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcg013a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
