\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar weakens to 11-year low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeffrey Hodgson \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 1997\par 23:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Dec 11 (Reuters) - Canada's dollar tumbled below 70 U.S. cents on Thursday for the first time in 11 years, battered by financial turmoil in Asia and a showdown between \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders and the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened to 69.98 U.S. cents, its weakest point since early 1986, before recovering slightly in the afternoon to end the North American trading session at 70.09 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's all-time low against the U.S. dollar was 69.13 U.S. cents in February 1986. \par \par \uc2  The sell-off occurred despite heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which bought Canadian dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in an attempt to halt the decline. \par \par \uc2  "The pronounced weakness today I think you can trace to the renewed turmoil in Asia. It's as much a (flight to the) U.S. dollar story as a Canadian dollar story," said Doug Porter, senior economist at brokerage Nesbitt Burns. \par \par \uc2  Many international investors have responded to the Asian crisis by moving their capital into so-called safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the U.S. dollar and German mark. More marginal \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like Canada's have been overlooked. \par \par \uc2  The Asian problems have also lowered expectations for growth in that region and hurt commodity prices. Canada is a major exporter of commodities like grain and metals and its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tends to mirror movements in global commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's slide is being partly blamed on the Bank of Canada's reluctance to raise interest rates. Rate hikes tend to strengthen a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by attracting capital from other countries. \par \par \uc2  After slashing interest rates to 40-year lows last year, the Bank of Canada has enacted just three rate hikes of 25 points each this year. \par \par \uc2  The most recent increase was on Nov. 25, when the bank raised the key bank rate to 4 percent. That move failed to arrest the dollar's decline. The bank rate is similar to the U.S. federal funds rate, which is at 5.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada has been reluctant to raise rates for fear of stalling the country's economic recovery. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, who expected more tightening, have sold off the dollar in the absence of more rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  "At the end of the day (the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness) centers on the credibility of the Bank of Canada," said Andrew Spence, senior economist with Deutsche Morgan Grenfell. \par \par \uc2  "They had got the market set up to look for a fairly steady tightening in interest rates..and the bank wasn't really willing to deliver," he said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said another Bank of Canada rate increase was needed to halt the dollar's slide and that it could occur within days. \par \par \uc2  "It is impossible for the bank now to step back and totally ignore what is happening. I think the odds are basically for a 50-basis-point tightening," said Andrew Pyle, senior economist with ABN-AMRO Bank Canada. \par \par \uc2  "The market knows that you cannot defend levels on the Canadian dollar with \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It may work for five minutes, but it never works (in the long run)," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par 