\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: Cdn Dlr Up Slighty As Major Data Loom \b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 1997\par 22:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar moved up slightly on Friday as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  players bided their time and began turning their attention to next week and its heavy data flow. \par \par \uc2  'The markets relatively thin today,' said Craig Wright, assistant chief economist at the Royal Bank of Canada. 'Probably people are a little cautious heading into next week. We get a bunch of key numbers starting on Monday and ending on Friday.' \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4226 at 4:47 p.m. EST (2147 GMT), up from C$1.4218 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT), but down from C$1.4238 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  While market players said trading was light on Friday, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  nonetheless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling the U.S. dollar when it hit a level of C$1.4245. The BOC normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when the U.S. dollar moves 70 ticks in either direction from the previous day's close. Dealers say the move (around 12:00 p.m EST) suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is uncomfortable with the weak state of the Canadian dollar and is prepared to defend it. \par \par \uc2  'They were definitely trying to make a point and sent a strong signal,' said a dealer with a major Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  Wright said the release of the third quarter current account deficit Monday will be an important signpost for the beleaguered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If the figure is C$15 billion or more - as many expect - that will signal that Canada's net external indebtedness as a share of GDP will be on the rise again, reversing the recent downward trend, he said. \par \par \uc2  'The figures will likely suggest we are borrowing again from the rest of the world, raising the issue about what sort of interest rate we will need to attract those lenders,' said Al Brownridge, vice-president of bonds with Investors Group. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 28-11-97 \par \par \uc2  2148GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtbs0964o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
