\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Stuck At Lows Not Seen In Nearly 3 Yrs \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 November 1997\par 22:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar was virtually unchanged Thursday, staying stuck at the lows reached on Wednesday as trading ground to a standstill because of the U.S. Thanksgiving Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  'I think Canada right now is just very bound to the lack of activity in the market,' said a trader at a major Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4246 at 4:40 p.m. EST (2140 GMT), from C$1.4244 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT) and from C$1.4248 late Wednesday. The U.S. dollar reached C$1.4265 in overnight trading, its highest point since hitting C$1.4270 in January of 1995. It's all-time high was C$1.4480 hit in February 1986. \par \par \uc2  'The move was pretty much overnight after the Bank stopped being in the market and Tokyo opened,' the trader said. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Wednesday after the U.S. dollar rose from its Tuesday close of C$1.4170 to C$1.4245. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its bank rate 1/4 point to 4.00% on Tuesday, but move failed to provide a sustained boost to the battered Canadian dollar. 'I think it was just too little, too late, and the whole market knew that,' the trader said. \par \par \uc2  When trading resumes in earnest next week, there will likely be more downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. 'From what I think, in the short term, there's no reason to hold Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 27-11-97 \pa