\par\par \b \uc2  Bk Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: More  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen Likely \b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 1997\par 17:33\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in defense of the ailing\par Canadian dollar Wednesday, selling U.S. dollars around\par 11:05 a.m. EST (1605 GMT), according to a trader.\par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness results despite a 1/4-point increase\par in the bank rate Tuesday - putting it up to 4.0% - designed\par specifically to provide the Canadian dollar some support.\par However, many dealers say the move merely provided \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par speculators a clear target to attack.\par    The bank tends to step into the market against moves\par in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of 70 ticks or more from the previous day's\par close. The U.S. dollar finished Tuesday at C$1.4170.\par    Analysts say Canadian dollar troubles stem directly from\par continued troubles in the Asian financial markets. There's\par concern a prolonged upheaval in the major economies of\par South Korea and Japan will put long-term downward pressure\par on commodity prices, an important factor in the Canadian\par economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    "What's driving it here is a continued spillover from\par the Asian crisis and a spillover from that to commodity\par prices," says Mark Chandler, senior economist with Goldman\par Sachs Canada. "We saw a little bit of an indication of that\par with the producer prices today."\par    The Canadian Industrial Product Price Index fell 0.3% in\par October, with the most important price declines in pork,\par softwood, lumber, aluminum products and refined zinc, said\par Statistics Canada earlier Wednesday.\par    "If you take a look at where the declines came you had\par a fall in the primary metals and the lumber, and that fed\par through to a weaker than expected increase in the producer\par prices," says Chandler. He says it's the first concrete\par sign of an effect from Asia on Canada's terms of trade, and\par underpins the Canadian dollar's weakness.\par    Some dealers have already begun to target U.S. dollar\par levels at all-time highs in the C$1.4400 and C$1.4500 area,\par not seen since 1986. Analysts say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will\par have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continually if the situation does not\par stabilize.\par    At 11:22 a.m. EST (1622 GMT), the U.S. dollar was still\par trading just above C$1.4240, and dealers say continued\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely this session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  11-26-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1133EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4020 4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtbq02lba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
