\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds, T-bills sell off at close. \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds and T-bills sold off by Wednesday's close after Governor Gordon Thiessen signalled that the Bank of Canada may further hike short-term interest rates to stop the slide in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Economist Richard Wylie at Toronto-Dominion Securities said a few investors were able to push long bond prices down in an illiquid market after Thiessen began speaking at around 1530 EST/ 2030 GMT. Flows were light ahead of tomorrow's U.S. Thanksgiving holiday which will close down all U.S. markets. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond fell C$0.67 to C$128.04 to yield 5.968 percent. \par \par \uc2  By comparison, the U.S. long bond rose 3/32 to yield 6.05 percent. The spread between the bonds narrowed to 8 basis points from 12 basis points at Tuesday's close. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. market closed at 1400 EST/1900 GMT today. \par \par \uc2  Wylie said weakness in the short end, due to a slipping Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , permeated the long end. \par \par \uc2  In comments to the federal Finance Committee on Wednesday, Thiessen acknowledged the weakness in the Canadian dollar which ended at C$1.4245 (US$0.7020) despite heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I guess there's some continued concern that if the Canadian dollar persists at these levels, the Bank of Canada will be forced to do again what it did yesterday," Wylie said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when-issued T-bill softened to yield 3.85 percent from 3.66 percent at the close of trading on Tuesday. In other prices, the 10-year Canadian government bond futures contract trading on the Montreal Exchange rose 0.13 to 122.85 with 4,209 contracts changing hands. A total of 14,319 contracts changed hands on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Lydia Zajc, Toronto newsroom, (416) 941-8109 or lydia.zajc@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbq022uj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
