\par\par \b \uc2  Canada markets falter on sliding dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeffrey Hodgson \par \uc2 514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 1997\par 22:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Nov 26 (Reuters) - Canadian financial markets slumped on Wednesday after the Canadian dollar fell to 2-1/2-year lows despite aggressive efforts by the Bank of Canada to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Stocks, T-bills and much of the bond market softened on concerns the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would weaken further and force the Bank of Canada to hike interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  "Given the difference in action between the Dow (Jones industrial average) and the TSE (Toronto Stock Exchange), there's obviously a lack of stability in our market," said Philip Strathy, chief investment officer with Strathy Investments Ltd. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark Toronto Stock Exchange 300 Index, which tumbled as much as 2 percent at midday, closed the session lower, ending down about 125.12 points at 6,505.40. \par \par \uc2  Pulling the market lower were gold stocks and interest-sensitive banking shares. While gold producers weakened because the price of gold fell to its lowest level in 12-1/2 years, concerns about the further Canadian interest rate hikes hurt banks and much of the rest of the market, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Causing the overall decline in Canadian markets was the weakened state of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Canada's dollar has been in a steady decline for several weeks as the financial crisis in Asia spilled over into North America. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still reacting to external factors and fears that the Japanese might repatriate Canadian and U.S. assets," said Rob Palombi, a senior fixed-income analyst with financial analysis firm MMS International. \par \par \uc2  In an attempt to halt the dollar's slide, the Bank of Canada on Tuesday raised its key bank rate, which is similar to the U.S. federal funds rate, by a quarter of a percentage point to 4.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it raised interest rates for the third time this year to put an end to what it called "persistent weakness" in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the Bank of Canada tightening had little effect, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling to a new 2-1/2-year low of C$1.4253 to the U.S. dollar overnight. \par \par \uc2  The continued slide in the dollar brought the Bank of Canada into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly bought Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "more aggressive" than usual and the bank was trying to sent a strong signal to the markets. \par \par \uc2  Weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is feeding speculation that the Bank of Canada will have to raise rates again soon to lure capital flows back into Canadian dollar assets. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of the scenario is pretty similar to what was happening in '94 and '95 with the Mexican peso crisis. You saw the same type of stuff going on then with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  raising rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily," said Harvinder Kalirai, Canadian analyst with New York-based financial analysis firm I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness reflects "a loss of investor confidence in Canadian assets," Kalirai said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtbq022ui\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
