\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends down on weaker employment data. \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Nov 7 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended weaker on Friday at C$1.4095 (US$0.7095) from Thursday's close of C$1.3987 (US$0.7149), after Canada reported lower than expected employment growth for October. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid after Statistics Canada reported employment fell by 11,100 positions versus expectations for a rise of 37,000, and the Bank of Canada did not deliver an anticipated interest rate rise. \par \par \uc2  "It's definitely down because of that and the weak employment numbers," said Mario Angastiniotis, economist at MMS International. \par \par \uc2  The unemployment numbers stunned economists, who had predicted the jobless rate would drop below the psychologically important 9 percent level for the first time in more than seven years. \par \par \uc2  "Let's face it, all of us were expecting 8.9 or 9 percent unemployment, so we were extremely surprised," said David Ebata, senior Canadian analyst with Technical Data in Boston. \par \par \uc2  "There are some very disturbing trends in the jobs data, especially when you look at the overall drop in employment," said Ebata, who added that economists could start scaling down their 1998 forecasts for Canada's economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp decline after the job numbers were reported prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early morning trade, buying Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars around the C$1.4075 (US$0.7104) level. \par \par \uc2  There were market rumors that the Bank of England \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last night to prop up the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the decline in employment puts the Bank of Canada in a difficult position. A weak dollar gives the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less economic ammunition to argue that a rate hike to defend the dollar is also a move to stave off inflationary pressures due to rapid growth. \par \par \uc2  "I think (the numbers) take the Bank of Canada out of the picture today. In fact, politically it may not be possible to raise interest rates. If they do, I would be quite impressed," said Michael Gregory, economist and strategist with Lehman Brothers in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, Canada's dollar fell to 1.2113 marks from Thursday's 1.2309 marks, but rose to 88.14 yen from 87.91 yen. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto Bureau 416 941-8100)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtb702ub7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
