\par\par \b \uc2  Canada's Martin keeps close watch on markets. \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  OTTAWA, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Canadian Finance Minister Paul Martin said he is keeping a close eye on the market turmoil that has sent panic through Canadian stocks and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We are monitoring the situation, and remain in contact with the Bank of Canada, market officials and the U.S. Treasury," Martin said in a statement released late Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian economy is in a position of strength, with sound economic fundamentals and the best fiscal health of the G7," Martin said, reiterating comments he made on Monday to reporters. "I feel very good about the Canadian situation and indeed, about the North American situation." \par \par \uc2  Martin indicated he had confidence in the safety mechanisms that shut down the Toronto Stock Exchange on Monday during late afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  "Trading and settlement mechanisms and market safety measures all worked well," Martin said in his statement. \par \par \uc2  The TSE, battered by heavy selling through the day, closed early after its key 300 index fell 434.29 points to 6599.20, mirroring losses on the New York Stock Exchange's Dow Jones index, which fell 554.26 points and was closed 30 minutes early at 7161.15. \par \par \uc2  The drop was the largest loss of points ever for the TSE. The index lost 6.17 percent on the day. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was hammered on Monday too, and opened much weaker again on Tuesday morning despite buying overnight by the Bank of England on behalf of the Bank of Canada. The Canadian dollar was trading at C$1.4119 (US$0.7083) early on Tuesday after closing at a two-year low of C$1.4050 (US$0.7117) on Monday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in aggressive attempts to buoy up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  ((Heather Scoffield, Reuters Ottawa Bureau 613 235-6745)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtas03eyv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
