\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Toronto stocks post biggest-ever point drop. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gilbert Le Gras \par \uc2 539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1997\par 00:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The Toronto Stock Exchange posted its biggest one-day point loss in history on Monday, as Canada's biggest equities market was walloped by stock losses in Asia and a 12-year low for gold prices. \par \par \uc2  Toronto's benchmark 300 Composite Index tumbled 434.29 points or 6.17 percent to 6599.20. The drop shattered the previous point loss record of 400.37 points set on "Black Monday," on Oct. 19, 1987. \par \par \uc2  Volume was 122.6 million shares, worth C$2.14 billion ($1.52 billion). Officials shut down trading one-half hour early at 3:30 P.M. EST (2030 GMT) because of the losses. Toronto trading was also suspended briefly earlier in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  In New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average also posted its biggest ever single-day loss, falling 554.26 points or 7.18 percent to 7161.15. Trading was also halted before the regular 4 P.M. EST (2100 GMT) close on the New York Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  In percentage terms, it was Toronto's fourth biggest ever decline. The record percentage loss was 11.13 percent on Black Monday in 1987. \par \par \uc2  "We haven't got over the Asian flu yet. We're being hit by Asia and the idea the Swiss might sell off their gold," said Fred Ketchen, senior vice president at Toronto brokerage ScotiaMcLeod. \par \par \uc2  Losses in Toronto were aggravated by the plunge in gold to $311.80 an ounce on Monday, its lowest price since July 8, 1985. Toronto's 300 Index has a heavy resource weighting. \par \par \uc2  Toronto's gold and precious metals index fell 770.06 points or 9.57 percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Gold traders said the market overreacted to a Swiss proposal last Friday to sell 1,400 tonnes of gold. The plan must be approved by parliament and a referendum in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Jack Geller, acting chairman of the Ontario Securities Commission, said that his agency was "closely monitoring the situation and will continue to do so in the morning when markets reopen." \par \par \uc2  Geller said his agency, which regulates stock trading in Toronto, Canada's biggest equities market, consulted through the day with regulators elsewhere in Canada and in the United States. \par \par \uc2  Montreal-based equities strategist Bill Ram said that "everybody's got to start making new computer models of global growth because of these low \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Far East." \par \par \uc2  Weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in southeast Asia threaten to cut import demand for basic commodities. \par \par \uc2  The market rout also sent the Canadian dollar tumbling to a two-year closing low of 71.33 U.S. cents despite at least three rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The only winners on the day were Canadian long bonds, which rallied as tumbling North American stock markets eased concerns that central banks would hike short-term interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "It's like a bungee cord market," said Hank Cunningham, director of fixed-income at First Marathon Securities Ltd. \par \par \uc2  "This morning investors were worried the U.S. Federal Reserve would increase rates, but after today's stunning selloff in North American stock markets, rate hikes would further scare equities players," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtar03lod\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
