\par\par \b \uc2  =Turbulent Global Markets Could Hasten Rate Hike In Canada \b0\par\par\uc2  By Eric Van Zant \par \uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1997\par 20:09\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Tumultuous trading in global\par financial markets is helping to unsettle the Canadian dollar\par and could hasten the next rate increase from the Bank of\par Canada, analysts say.\par    The wide gap between lower yielding Canadian debt\par instruments and their U.S. counterparts and a recent turn\par for the worse in Canada's balance of payments leaves the\par country's dollar much weaker and vulnerable to the unsettled\par trading now gripping global stock and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\par    As Southeast Asia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  troubles reached the Hong\par Kong dollar last week, they sparked a precipitous plunge in\par Hong Kong stocks that rippled through the world's major\par stock exchanges and many of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The turmoil\par initially boosted the Canadian dollar, but traders say that\par since then there's been some selling associated with the\par action.\par    "We are getting awfully close to a rate hike in this\par country," said Michael Gregory, fixed-income economist with\par Lehman Brothers Canada.\par    The U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar surged against its Canadian counterpart\par Monday, touching C$1.3980 and prompting the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell U.S. dollars. Further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be required to keep a lid on the U.S.\par dollar's rise, observers said. At mid-afternoon, the U.S.\par dollar was trading at C$1.3997, up from C$1.3918 at Friday's\par close.\par    That's because losses by the Canadian dollar leave\par monetary conditions as loose as they were on Sept. 30, the\par day before the Bank of Canada last raised its bank rate, by\par 1/4 point to 3.75%. As part of an export-driven economy, the\par Canadian dollar's trading level is an important determinant\par of the level of economic stimulus and growth. The U.S.\par dollar hit a low of C$1.3690 after the rate increase.\par    Central bankers signaled at that time a strong desire\par for less stimulative monetary conditions, so much so that\par analysts say if their words are to remain credible, the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will have to bump rates up again to strengthen\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and tighten credit conditions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (MORE) DOW JONES NEWS  10-27-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1409EST\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4020 4224 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dtar029z6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
