\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Bonds End Lower As Canadian Dlr Bashes Short End \b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1997\par 23:16\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Canadian bonds ended just a little lower Tuesday afternoon with a Treasurys market that was also moderately lower following the announcement the Federal Reserve will not be raising interest rates. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Few market observers said they thought there would be any change in\par interest rate policy emerging from Tuesday's Federal Open Market\par Committee meeting, but when the announcement was made the market crept\par mostly down as small bouts of profit taking occured.\par    4.00s 1999  99.79  dn 0.15  4.13% vs 4.03%\par    7.00s 2001 106.40  dn 0.20  5.22% vs 5.17%\par    7.25s 2007 109.41  dn 0.19  5.96% vs 5.94%\par    8s    2027 119.00  dn 0.35  6.54% vs 6.52%\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-yr: -0.25 vs -0.26\par    CGB SEP 97 120.16  dn 0.21  vol. 3,129\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Shorter-term Canadian bonds, meanwhile, are losing ground after being boosted by a more stable Canadian dollar. The Canadian dollar slipped after the announcement earlier in the day that core inflation slowed to 1.5% year-over-year in July, from 2.0%. \par \par \uc2  That was broadly interpreted to mean the Bank of Canada is a while from its next boost in interest rates. The U.S. dollar climbed from the C$1.3880 level before the announcement to the C$1.3940 level where the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell U.S. dollars and subdue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb. \par \par \uc2  Jim Webber, managing director of of fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. said the market is just very unsure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate policy right now. "The market no longer feels confident it knows what the bank's plans are." He said the market consensus seemed to be for more tightening than now seems likely, and everything is on hold until more economic indicators build up. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian one year treasury bills are also being hurt by a combination\par of the weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and a rebound from supply-driven lower yields\par that hung over the market in the past couple of weeks. One analyst said\par there had been an attempt to squeeze the one-year market, but the\par pressure was obviously begining to ease. The bills is currently trading\par around 4.09% from 3.80% late Monday.\par    -By Norm Barnett\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  08-19-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1716EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1182 38700 4020 4118 4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dt8j01r40\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
