\par\par \b \uc2  =Bank Of Canada -2: Current-Account Deficit Seen A Problem \b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 1997\par 21:50\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Nonethless, Ted Carmichael, economist with J.P. Morgan Securities \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , played down the meaning of Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is certainly watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  action carefully, "but I don't think the BOC is at all panicked about it," Carmichael said. \par \par \uc2  He said Canadian dollar losses could be traced in part to a U.S. dollar downturn against the the yen. The dollar slipped Wednesday amid speculation that the Bank of Japan could tighten monetary policy sooner than many had expected. \par \par \uc2  With Canadian inflation at just 1.5% in May, down from April's 1.7%, the BOC can't be too worried about the inflationary effects of stronger growth, Carmichael added. "One doesn't get the feeling that they are in a hurry to rebalance the MCI," he said. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, rates certainly aren't flagging an imminent move in policy: indeed, the three-month rate, at 2.87%, remains well below the upper limit of the Bank of Canada's target range for overnight funds of 2.75%-3.25%. \par \par \uc2  And such low short-term interest rates have created a problem for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year. The three-month rate is 220 basis points below its U.S. counterpart - hardly an incentive for investors to buy Canadian securities. "The Canadian dollar has been the disappointment of the year," said Nesbitt's Rosenberg. \par \par \uc2  What's more, as lower interest rates have stimulated demand, they've led to a huge increase in imports. Import growth topping 18% on the year in April - triple the expansion in exports - could lead to a current-account deficit of C$10.0 billion in the second quarter, Rosenberg said. And that hasn't yet been priced into the market, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar rally in 1996 was seen by many as a reflection of a move to a current-account surplus late in the year. \par \par \uc2  Under normal circumstances the strength of the domestic economy would be a bullish signal for the Canadian dollar, he said. "But it isn't, because growth is strong enough to boost consumer spending and imports, but not enough to pull the Bank of Canada off the sidelines." \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS  06-25-97 \par \par \uc2  1550EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 4222 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dt6p01cn0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
