\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Seeks To Smooth Sharp C$ Fall \b0\par\par\uc2 228 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 1997\par 16:55\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Friday to sell U.S. dollars starting\par around C$1.3847 at 10:39 a.m. EDT (1439 GMT), in an effort\par to smooth a sharp decline in the Canadian dollar.\par    Canada's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came under heavy pressure after\par relatively strong U.S. employment numbers for May raised\par anticipation of a U.S. rate increase at the July 2 Federal\par Reserve meeting. The U.S. dollar closed Thursday at\par C$1.3773 but began rising quickly as the North American\par session got underway Friday - the Bank of Canada leans\par against moves of 70 ticks or greater as a matter of routine\par in an effort to keep volatility down.\par    While many believe the Bank of Canada itself will raise\par interest rates sometime in the second half, the move is not\par seen so urgent as a hike in U.S. rates. Canadian short-term\par interest rates are already well below their U.S. counterparts\par and a U.S. move would leave Canadian rates that much less\par attractive.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not seek to target any specific\par trading levels or to change the direction of trade, as\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official have stated repeatedly in the past.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES NEWS  06-06-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1055EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4