\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Canada dollar rallies on bank comments. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Mai 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, May 15 (Reuter) - Canada's dollar rose almost one U.S. cent on Thursday in its most volatile session since Quebec's 1995 sovereignty referendum, after Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen suggested the need for a shift to a less stimulative monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rocketed to C$1.3710 (US$0.7293) in late trading after opening the North American session at C$1.3846 (US$0.7222). The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed on Wednesday at C$1.3893 (US$0.7197). \par \par \uc2  The rally prompted repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the day by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which began selling Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars at the C$1.3828 (US$0.7231) level. \par \par \uc2  "The bank's been in everywhere. They're coming in every five or 10 points," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar began to rise after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  released its semi-annual monetary policy report, which indicated the bank's willingness to shift to a more neutral monetary policy from a stimulative one if inflation remained stable. \par \par \uc2  Strong fundamentals in the economy, low inflation and improvments in Canada's fiscal policy "continue to underpin market expectations of a future appreciation of the Canadian dollar," the report said. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen confirmed the bank's confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he told a news conference, "I really believe the underlying trend is upward and I know that on balance it has tended to be weak recently after the American interest rate move." \par \par \uc2  The prospect that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stop resisting future U.S. interest rate hikes had an immediate effect on the dollar as investors snapped up heavy volumes of Canadian bonds and securities. \par \par \uc2  Canadian bonds, which typically trade in the same direction as U.S. treasuries, were sharply higher today, while U.S. treasuries slumped. \par \par \uc2  "The idea that the bank is going to switch to a less stimulative monetary policy comes as a relief to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst for MMS International. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has depreciated by five percent since January, due mostly to concerns over diverging policies between the U.S. Federal Reserve and Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This rally is all about what is going right for the Canadian economy," Palombi said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said that early morning U.S. data indicating largely stable consumer prices helped prepare the Canada dollar for its impressive showing. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Labor Department reported that the monthy Consumer Price Index (CPI) increased 0.1 percent in April, as forecast last week by a Reuters survey of economists. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt5f05g74\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
