\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Afternoon: C$ Surges To Life Causing BOC  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Mai 1997\par 23:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar came to life Thursday, surging against its U.S. counterpart and requiring to smoothing rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3875 at 4:59 p.m. EDT (2059 GMT), down from C$1.3955 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.3975 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Essentially building on strength in the bond market, the Canadian dollar began rising in the early session, eventually forcing the U.S. dollar to fall through stop-loss sell levels at C$1.3950, C$1.3910 and C$1.3880. \par \par \uc2  The impetus came, however, with Tuesday's news that the first-quarter employment cost index was up just 0.6%, well below expectations of 0.8%. The data cooled expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve would have to raise interest rates much more in the current cycle, lowering one of the main obstacles to Canadian dollar strength. \par \par \uc2  'The big negative in the market was the fact that the Fed tightened (in March) and was expected to tighten further later in May and that is now up in the air,' said John Nicholson, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading with Scotia Capital Markets. 'With Fed policy (possibly) on hold it is an environment where the Canadian dollar can and should do better,' he explained. (see story on Dow Jones page 4233 for an analysis of the day's action). \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  first at C$1.3885, buying U.S. dollars around midday, and again at C$1.3870. \par \par \uc2  Near-term direction could now depend on the story told by April employment figures in the U.S., due at 8:30 a.m. EDT Friday. A excessively strong number could quickly reintroduce U.S. rate fears and reverse market fortunes. \par \par \uc2  But Nicholson said the U.S. dollar appears to have moved resolutely into a more neutral range roughly between C$1.3750 and C$1.3925. He cautioned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could remain vulnerable to trouble as the Canadian election campaign kicks into gear - voters go to the polls on June 2. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:59 p.m. EDT (2059 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT) and late Wednesday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.3875      1.3955     1.3975\par    CDN/DEM     1.2411      1.2357     1.2379\par    CDN/YEN     91.21       90.99      90.91\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 01-05-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2105GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt51034gs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
