\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -5: Poland  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2 238 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1997\par 00:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  In other developments Tuesday: \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's CPI was up 0.2% in March from February, and up 2.0% from March 1996. Excluding food and energy, Canada's CPI in March was up 1.7% from last March. \par \par \uc2  Canada's retail sales, excluding autos, were up 1.3% in February from January. Including autos, retail sales were up 1.1%. \par \par \uc2  Dutch \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Wim Duisenberg said he expects economic growth in the Netherlands of 2.8% in 1997 and 3.25% in 1998, slightly below forecasts from the government's central planning bureau. \par \par \uc2  Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second consecutive day, traders said, buying dollars at a rate of 3.1345 zlotys. \par \par \uc2  New orders to Swedish industries fell 0.9% in February from January, but were 3.4% higher than in February 1996. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's export contracts received in March rose to $10.47 billion, up 5.6% from a year ago. The number was the second-highest level ever recorded. \par \par \uc2  The Czech trade deficit widened to 40.181 billion koruna at the end of the first quarter, from 27.118 billion koruna for the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's March CPI was up 5.7% year-on-year, from an 6.5% year-on-year increase in February. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 22-04-97 \par \par \uc2  2206GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 1865 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4m0316e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pn