\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  For Third-Straight Session \b0\par\par\uc2 278 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1997\par 21:57\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Canada stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Monday, selling U.S. dollars for the third straight session in an effort to support the weak Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders saw the bank selling U.S. dollars around the C$1.3995, up from C$1.3965 Friday and around C$1.3940 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Once again, the bank was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market much earlier than it would normally have - a pattern over the past week that says to many market observers that the bank is increasingly concerned about the fate of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said there continues to be demand for U.S. dollars despite the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and some are not convinced this latest round will halt the Canadian dollar's slide. \par \par \uc2  Ultimately, the target for the market is the C$1.40 level on the U.S. dollar, and only an interest-rate increase by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the Canadian dollar from weakening futher. \par \par \uc2  "The market would like to see it (a rate hike) as a signal the bank understands what's going on, and that they're actually in the game to provide some stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. \par \par \uc2  Although weakening since the beginning of the year, the Canadian dollar has come under more intense pressure since March 25, the day the U.S. Federal Reserve raised its key interest rate 25 basis points and the Bank of Canada declined to match it with a hike of its own. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS  04-14-97 \par \par \uc2  1557EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 4300 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011006dt4e00rv2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
