\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: BoC Steps In Again To Assist Cdn Dlr \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 1997\par 22:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar slipped once more against the U.S. dollar late Monday and ended below Friday's levels, prompting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf for the third time in as many days. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3997 at 4:40 p.m. EDT (2040 GMT), up from C$1.3980 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.3980 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen selling U.S. dollars for Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the C$1.3995 level. Analysts said the bank has been been trying to keep the U.S. dollar from making a major breach of the C$1.4000 figure. The bank stepped in last week around the C$1.3945 and C$1.3960 level, breaking with their unofficial policy of waiting for the U.S. dollar to move 0.70 Canadian cents from the previous day's close before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders attributed part of Monday's moves to market jitters about the U.S. consumer price index being released Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Although a break of that level seems inevitable, the bank broadly hinted it won't defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher interest rates, so analysts said it has been forced to spend its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in order to prop the Canadian dollar up. \par \par \uc2  One Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst in Boston said the interest rate differential is at the heart of the overall weakness, as foreign investors move away from countries like Japan where rates remain low. 'Lower yielding countries are getting hammered,' said the analyst. 'And Canada's one of them.' \par \par \uc2  Now that a Canadian election call for June 2 seems inevitable, analysts also say the likelihood of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  catching up to the Federal Reserve rates before then is diminished. 'They don't want to be seen as being political,' said the analyst \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:40 p.m. EDT (2040 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT) and late Friday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.3997      1.3980     1.3980\par    CDN/DEM     1.2367      1.2318     1.2303\par    CDN/YEN     90.41       90.44      90.13\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 14-04-97\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2044GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4e02of2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
