\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: Canadian Dlr Weakens Again On U.S. Data \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 1997\par 22:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar weakened off again Friday, prompting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf as investors sold it off on moderately strong U.S. data that suggests further U.S. interest-rate increases. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3980 at 4:40 p.m. EDT (2040 GMT), up from C$1.3930 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.3945 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  For the second session running the Bank of Canada stepped in to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Canadian dollars as the U.S. dollar took off. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved in Friday around C$1.3960 after having come in Thursday near the C$1.3945 level. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said market players were spooked by figures released by the U.S. Department of Commerce Friday morning. The department said revised retail sales figures for February showed a 1.8% increase from the previously reported 0.8%. March figures showed a jump of 0.5% for ex-automobile sales and a 0.2% rise in the headline rate. That was a little lower than was expected but still enough to impart the message of a strong trend in consumer spending for the first quarter of 1997. \par \par \uc2  The take on the figures by market observers is that this all but clinches another rise in U.S. interest rates by the Federal Reserve at their next policy meeting May 20. With the Bank of Canada demonstrating a reluctance to move their rates higher, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is being forced to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Steve Demers, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for the National Bank said the bank seems willing to accept a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but, 'they want to make sure that if the Canadian dollar does depreciate it does so in orderly fashion - and that's why they've \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower than some expected.' \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 11-04-97 \par \par \uc2  2055GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4b02p7n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
