\par\par \b \uc2  Battered Canadian dollar hits 2-year low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 1997\par 21:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, April 11 (Reuter) - The Canadian dollar was under siege on Friday, pounded to a two-year low by signs of rising U.S. inflation and signals that Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may resist hiking domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at C$1.3982 (71.52 U.S. cents) -- its lowest point since April 3, 1995 -- as investors unloaded the dollar in heavy selling. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the dollar and support it at the C$1.3960 (71.63 U.S. cents) level on Friday failed to stem the markets' strong anti-Canada bias. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is vulnerable right now. It could easily hit C$1.4000 (71.42 U.S. cents) before it rebounds," said John Anania, an economist with Royal Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  Friday's sell-off followed U.S. indicators showing inflation worries were reviving in the United States. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Commerce Department reported that the core producer price index rose by a higher than expected 0.4 percent in March, with retail sales growing 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. inflationary pressures could push the Federal Reserve to hike interest rates again in May and force the Bank of Canada to raise its rates in defense of the flagging Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  A 25-basis-point rate hike by the Fed on March 25 widened the spread between U.S. and Canadian short-term rates to more than 200 points, pressuring the Canadian dollar and renewing calls for the Bank of Canada to follow suit. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar has shed almost four percent of its value against the U.S. dollar since January as investors moved to buy the higher yielding U.S. short-term securities. \par \par \uc2  "Canada (the dollar) is going to get hurt in this environment of rising U.S. interest rates," said William Chin, vice-president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with ABN-AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  He added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to raise interest rates, now at 30-year lows after 11 interest rate cuts last year. \par \par \uc2  Early last year, the Bank of Canada began pursuing a "made in Canada" interest rate policy aimed at revitalising Canada's lackluster economy. The bank's rate slashing last year was independent of action by the Fed. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Deputy Governor Timothy Noel may have tipped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand on Thursday when he told Reuters in Lima, Peru that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not react to "every wiggle" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The bank does not want to react to every wiggle in the dollar," said Noel, adding that the bank would raise interest rates only if monetary conditions warranted such a move. \par \par \uc2  Noel's comments triggered a slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar on Thursday, forcing the bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor Charles Freedman appeared to step back from those remarks on Friday when he said at a conference in New York that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept close tabs on exchange rate movements. \par \par \uc2  But some economists said the beating of the drums for a Canadian interest rate hike flew in the face of Canada's economic situation. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Canada is focused on deflation. A cheaper Canadian dollar helps them with that battle by easing overall monetary conditions and by pushing domestic prices up," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist with High Frequency Economics, in a commentary released on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The persistent challenging of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do something about the falling dollar "is getting boring," Weinberg said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4b07745\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
