\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian bonds close weaker amid drop in  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 478 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, April 10 (Reuter) - Canadian bonds closed weaker on Thursday, underperforming U.S. treasuries as a sharp drop in the value of the Canadian dollar to two-year lows and a softer U.S. bond market weighed on prices, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We're starting to see slightly more aggressive selling into the close," said Andrew Pyle, head of fixed-income research with ABN-AMRO Bank Canada. "It's the weakness in the Canadian dollar and if you look to the U.S. curve, things improved a little bit, but the tone is still weak." \par \par \uc2  Canada's 30-year benchmark bond fell C$0.40 to C$107.00 to yield 7.390 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 3/32 to yield 7.11 percent. The spread between the two widened to 28 basis points from 25 basis points at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  Canadian and U.S. bonds began the session lower as investors looked ahead nervously to the release of Friday's U.S. economic data. The U.S. retail sales and producer price reports for March were expected to provided further clues about whether the U.S. Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee would hike rates again when it met in May. \par \par \uc2  But Canadian bonds were also hit by a drop in the Canadian dollar to a two-year low later in the session. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar weakened after comments from a Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  official led to a selloff and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Canadian dollars to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it hit a new two-year low at C$1.3945 (US$0.7171). \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Deputy Governor Tim Noel told Reuters in Lima, Peru that the Bank would not react to "every wiggle" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggesting it had no plans to raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of players here in the domestic market are going to be factoring in a possible move up to the the C$1.40 level. If that's the case, then you're going to see the entire Canadian curve really underperform the U.S., regardless of what kind of data comes out tomorrow morning," said Pyle. \par \par \uc2  In other prices, the Government of Canada's 10-year benchmark bond fell C$0.30 to C$100.80 to yield 6.884 percent while the U.S. bond fell 2/32 to yield 6.91 percent. \par \par \uc2  The negative spread between the bonds narrowed to three basis points from six basis points at the close of trading on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The three-month cash T-bill yielded 3.17 percent against its U.S. equivalent which yielded 5.28 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Jeffrey Hodgson (416) 941-8105, e-mail: jeffrey.hodgson@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011002dt4a074vc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
